Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 1 of 9
MEMORANDUM OF UNDERSTANDING BETWEEN
LARAMIE COUNTY SCHOOL DISTRICT NUMBER ONE AND
THE CITY OF CHEYENNE, WYOMING

1.
Parties. This Memorandum of Understanding ("MOU") is made and entered into by and
between Laramie County School District Number 1 ("District"), whose address is 2810
House Avenue, Cheyenne, WY 82001, and the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming, ("City")
whose address is 415 W. 18th Street, Cheyenne, Wyoming 82001.

2.
Purpose. The purpose of this MOU is to delineate the terms in which the City will
provide police services to District schools within the recognized area of jurisdiction of
the City, school-related events during the academic year, including summer school of
each year (Academic Year), under the School Resource Officer(s) Program (SRO). The
primary charge of the SRO Program is the reduction, prevention, and responsiveness to
school-related violence and crime. The SRO Program creates and maintains a safe,
secure, and crime-free learning environment for students, educators and the District. In
addition, the SRO Program is designed to develop and enhance rapport between youth,
parents, the surrounding school community, police officers, school leaders, and staff.

3.
Term of MOU. This MOU shall commence upon the day and date last signed and
executed by the duly authorized representatives of the parties to this MOU and shall
remain in full force and effect until August 31, 2025. This MOU shall specifically
supersede any prior MOU entered into by the Parties and in effect at the commencement
of this MOU.

4.
Payment. The District agrees to pay the City for the services described herein.
Specifically, the District shall pay as follows:

A.
All costs of salary and benefits incurred by the City in the performance of duties
under this Agreement shall be paid by the City, and the District shall reimburse
the City for all such costs.

B.
Said sum shall be invoiced to the District monthly and in arrears.

5.
Responsibilities of District.

A.
The District agrees school requested SRO attendance at events occurring outside
of normal school hours or outside the Academic Year constitute "Additional
Costs."

B.
The District agrees that SRO attendance at events occurring outside of normal
school hours or outside the Academic Year are at the mutual agreement of the
District and City. The District may flex an SRO’s schedule to assist at such events
and the flex will be taken in accordance with City Police Department Policies
and Procedures.
7:07 pm, May 20 2024
```

### Page 2 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 2 of 9

C.
The District shall designate and provide the City with contact information for a
District point of contact responsible for coordinating the SRO program. The point
of contact will be the District Safety and Security Program Administrator, Phone
# 307-771-2231.

D.
The District agrees to coordinate with the City and share information to the extent
allowed by law.

E.
The District agrees that it is responsible for student discipline for behaviors
occurring on District property or at District activities.

F.
The District acknowledges that violations of District or school policies, student
discipline issues, or other matters that do not rise to the level of criminal activity
remain the general purview of the District. Accordingly, student behavioral
problems or similar events, unless criminal in nature or posing a threat of
imminent physical danger to the student or others, will not generally result in the
use of police force to restrain, detain, or otherwise restrict the activities of a
student or students engaged in such activity. The District agrees that it shall not
involve the SRO in school discipline matters unless school or public safety is at
risk or reporting is required by law.

G.
The District agrees that the school’s principal or principal’s designee, absent
emergency circumstances, will determine whether to involve SRO or City Police
enforcement in any matter.

H.
The District agrees that SRO or City Police referrals may be made for the
following:

(i)
Assaults or battery;

(ii)
Felony or violent criminal offenses, including but not limited to robbery,
extortion, arson, or sexual assault.
(iii)
Use or possession of dangerous weapons by a student, including but not
limited to knives, guns, and explosive devices;

(iv)
Sale, possession, or use of illegal drugs, tobacco/nicotine product, or
alcohol;

(v)
Threats of physical harm to school employees or the public; and

(vi)
Destruction, damage, and vandalism of District property.

I.
The District agrees that the SRO shall comply with City Police Department
Policies and Procedures; where City police policies conflict with District Policies
```

### Page 3 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 3 of 9
and Procedures, SRO shall defer to the City Police Department Policies and
Procedures.

J.
Except in emergency, urgent or preliminary public safety inquiry circumstances,
SROs shall follow these conditions for interviewing or searching students
suspected of criminal activity:

(i)
SROs may question a student about subjects that may result in or identify
a crime, threat or concern utilizing the following process. Unless an
emergency or urgent circumstance exists, any law enforcement officer
(LEO) desiring to question a student at school must notify the school
principal or the principal’s designee in advance so that permission for
questioning may be obtained from a parent or guardian of the child.

(ii)
Once notified that a parent or guardian asks that the student not be
questioned until he/she or his/her designee arrives, the principal will
advise the LEO and ask that the officer wait to question the student until
the parent or guardian arrives.

(iii)
If the parent or guardian cannot be reached, the student may be questioned
by the officer with the principal or the principal’s designee present. The
principal or his/her designee must be present when any law enforcement
interview of a child is conducted on school property.

(iv)
Pursuant to Wyoming Statute § 14-6-206(c), after issuing any citation to
a child for a violation of a state or federal law or a municipal ordinance
for which incarceration or a fine may be imposed, the law enforcement
agency issuing the citation, or its designee shall take reasonable actions
to notify the child’s parent, guardian, or custodian. SROs will only be
responsible for issuing citations related to violations of law, not violations
of school or district rules or policies.

(v)
If the parent or guardian is the subject of a potential abuse or neglect
allegation, neither the LEO nor the Department of Family Services (DFS)
is required to, nor may the principal or his/her designee, notify the parent
or guardian prior to a LEO and/or DFS questioning the child. In such
circumstances, the LEO and/or DFS representative shall question the
student with the principal or his/her designee present.

(vi)
If there are exigent or urgent circumstances that prevent prior parental
notification.

a)
Parent or guardian shall be notified as soon as reasonably
possible.
```

### Page 4 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 4 of 9
b)
School personnel will serve ‘in loco parentis’ (in place of a parent
or guardian).

(vii)
Absent exigent or urgent circumstances, a principal or designee shall be
consulted before the SRO conducts questioning of a student or searches
student’s person, possessions, or locker to allow time to express any
concerns about reasonableness and to notify parent or guardian.

(viii) When there is a probable cause to believe the student has committed or is
committing a felony offense or the offense at issue poses a threat of
serious and immediate injury, the SRO may conduct questioning or
search at school without notice to school personnel, parent, or guardian.

(ix)
SROs may not request that school officials conduct a search of the
student’s person, possessions, or locker to evade the probable cause
standard required of law enforcement.

(x)
Nothing herein shall prevent the SRO from engaging students in casual
conversation about subjects other than inquiries related to suspected
criminal activity.

K.
The District agrees that SRO audio and/or video recordings constitute “peace
officer recordings” under the Wyoming Public Records Act. Wyoming Statute §
16-4-201(xi). As such, SRO peace officer recordings are the sole property of the
City and may not be released except as permitted by law.

L.
The District will timely notify the SRO or the City Police of any student with
disabilities that require specialized treatment or accommodation regarding any
matter referred to the SRO by the District.

M.
The District shall provide each SRO with private and secure office space at the
appropriate school with access to technological support.

N.
The District may provide SRO access to all security camera systems.

O.
The District shall provide each SRO limited access to the District’s Infinite
Campus system to include student name, address, parent, or guardian contact
information, in accordance with local, state, and federal law. The SROs will be
required to complete annual Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act
(FERPA) training. The training will be required prior to or at the beginning of
the new school year and will be provided by the District at no cost to the City.
The District will maintain the SRO’s training records depicting the FERPA
training was completed. The SRO will not include any information from Infinite
Campus in any report or formal record of a law enforcement investigation.

P.
The District shall provide the SRO access to all school facilities for the purposes
of official duties.
