### Page 5 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 5 of 9

Q.
The District understands and agrees the SRO may be immediately recalled for
public emergencies. The parties agree that SRO recall for public emergencies is
not a material breach of this MOU and there is no penalty to the City.

6.
Responsibilities of the City.

A.
The City agrees to provide the District with Seven (7) SROs and one SRO
Supervisor for the 2024-2025 Academic Year.  The supervisor’s responsibilities
will be to manage and coordinate the City’s performance under this Agreement.

B.
The City agrees that SRO duties are to respond to and investigate criminal acts
on District property and/or at District events during normal school hours, or as
otherwise agreed to, during the Academic Year.

C.
The City agrees that the SRO may initiate appropriate law enforcement actions
to address criminal matters, including matters that threaten the safety and security
of the school or its occupants, and/or intervene with staff or students when
necessary to ensure the immediate safety of persons in the school environment in
light of an actual or imminent threat to health or safety. Any such intervention
shall be reasonably calculated to protect the physical safety of members within
the school community, while minimizing, to the extent possible, any unintended
negative effects on students. All law enforcement actions and interventions to
protect the safety of others shall be consistent with all applicable laws,
regulations, and policies.

D.
The City agrees that except for felony offenses or criminal offenses that
constitute a threat to the safety of the school or public (“Serious Offenses”), the
SRO will notify and consult with the school principal or his designee prior to
questioning a student.

E.
The City agrees that except for Serious Offenses, the SRO will delay questioning
a student until the parent or guardian arrives.

F.
The City agrees that except for Serious Offenses, the school principals or their
designees shall be present for student interviews and searches on District
property.

G.
The City will take reasonable action to notify the parent or guardian of any
student issued a citation. See Wyoming Statute § 14-6-206(c).

H.
The City agrees that the SROs may not request that school officials conduct a
search of the student’s person, possessions, or locker to avoid probable cause
standards.

I.
The City agrees that SRO may provide educational support, when applicable, for
in-service training for school staff and students on emergency preparedness,
```

### Page 6 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 6 of 9
crisis intervention, crime trends, law enforcement policies and intervention
methods.

J.
The City agrees that the school may request assistance of an SRO to conduct a
search of a student’s person, possessions, or locker on behalf of the school when
the school has a reasonable suspicion of a violation of school policy or law by
the student.  Any search will be conducted at the sole discretion of the SRO.

K.
The City understands that school district records and video may be protected as
confidential by federal and state laws and that such records cannot be accessed
without a warrant or other court order or without consent of the individual legally
authorized to give consent. If SROs require access to records, documents, or other
school district information, whether in hard copy, electronic, cloud-based, or any
other format, they will work with District personnel to lawfully obtain such
information.

L.
The City agrees to provide a replacement officer if the assigned SRO will be
absent for ten (10) or more consecutive school days. The officer will cover the
SRO’s duty assignment at the actual cost of the replacement officer. Further, the
district shall not be billed for periods when individual SROs are absent from their
assigned duties/school(s), to include periods of professional training for collateral
duty. The SRO is encouraged, when possible, to take personal leave during
scheduled school breaks.

7.
Special Provisions.

A.
SROs will be selected by means of a joint selection committee, comprised of
representatives from the District who will be appointed by the Superintendent of
schools and representatives from the City appointed by the Chief of Police. The
committee will make recommendations to the Chief of Police; however, final
selection of SRO is at the sole discretion of the City.

B.
SRO Training specific to law enforcement duties shall be provided and paid for
by the City. Training for SRO in areas that are specific to juvenile crimes and
SRO duties may be provided by the City and District. The District will provide
overview and familiarization with District techniques and procedures to SROs as
necessary and with content relevant to SRO duties.  The District’s Safety and
Security Program Administrator will coordinate dates and times of this training
with the SRO’s supervisor. Training requests will be made to the SRO’s
supervisor and the Superintendent of Schools or the District’s Safety and Security
Program Administrator.

C.
At least sixty (60) days prior to the expiration of this MOU, the City and the
District will jointly evaluate the SRO program.
```

### Page 7 of 10

```text
Memorandum of Understanding School Resource Officers Program

Page 7 of 9
D.
The City will evaluate the effectiveness of each SRO. The City shall seek input
from the District for each evaluation.

E.
SROs assigned to provide services under this Agreement shall, at all times, be
employees of the City.  The City shall comply with all federal, state, and local
laws and ordinances regarding the employment relationship between the City and
SROs assigned to provide services under this Agreement.

F.
Long Term Official School Closure: Should any non-weather-related event
require long-term official closure of school locations for more than five (5)
consecutive school days, the District may request limited law enforcement
services with the balance of SRO’s daily hours redirected to supervise schools
and/or released back to the City as best represent the interests of both the City
and the District.  If such interests cannot be agreed upon, this MOU shall be
suspended for the remainder of the period of the official closure, and payments
vacated for duties not performed during such period.

8.
General Provisions

A.
Amendments. Either party may request changes to this MOU. Any changes,
modifications, revisions, or amendments to this MOU which are mutually agreed
upon by the parties to this MOU shall be incorporated by written instrument,
executed, and signed by all parties to this MOU.

B.
Applicable Law. The construction, interpretation, and enforcement of this MOU
shall be governed by the laws of the State of Wyoming. The courts of the State
of Wyoming shall have jurisdiction over any action arising out of this MOU and
over the parties, and the venue shall be the First Judicial District, Laramie
County, Wyoming.

C.
Availability of Funds. Each payment obligation of either party is conditioned
upon the availability of government funds which are appropriated or allocated
for the payment of this obligation. If funds are not allocated and available for the
continuance of the services performed by either party, the MOU may be
terminated by either party at the end of the period for which the funds are
available. Each party shall notify the other party at the earliest possible time of
the services which will or may be affected by a shortage of funds. No penalty
shall accrue to either party in the event this provision is exercised, and neither
party shall be obligated or liable for any future payments due or for any damages
as a result of termination under this section. This provision shall not be construed
to permit either party to terminate this MOU to acquire similar services from
another entity.

D.
Entirety of Agreement. This MOU, consisting of nine (9) pages, and
Attachment A, Salary and Equipment Costs consisting of (1) page represents the
entire and integrated agreement between the parties and supersedes all prior
negotiations, representations, and agreements, whether written or oral.
